## Solution

We determine $a_i \bmod 100$ for all $i$, showing the sequence stabilizes.

**Order of 3 modulo 100.** Since $\gcd(3,100)=1$ and $\varphi(100)=40$, the order of $3$ divides $40$. Computing: $3^4=81$, $3^5\equiv 43$, $3^{10}\equiv 43^2=1849\equiv 49$, $3^{20}\equiv 49^2=2401\equiv 1\pmod{100}$. Checking proper divisors of $20$: $3^1,3^2,3^4,3^5,3^{10}\not\equiv 1\pmod{100}$. Hence $\operatorname{ord}_{100}(3)=20$, so
$$a_{i+1}=3^{a_i}\equiv 3^{a_i\bmod 20}\pmod{100}.$$

**Order of 3 modulo 20.** $\varphi(20)=8$. $3^4=81\equiv 1\pmod{20}$, and $3^2=9\not\equiv 1$, $3^1\not\equiv 1$, so $\operatorname{ord}_{20}(3)=4$. Thus
$$a_{i+1}\equiv 3^{a_i\bmod 4}\pmod{20}.$$

**$a_i$ modulo 4.** Since $3\equiv -1\pmod 4$ and every $a_i$ is odd (a power of $3$), $a_{i+1}=3^{a_i}\equiv (-1)^{a_i}\equiv -1\equiv 3\pmod 4$. With $a_1=3\equiv 3\pmod 4$, by induction $a_i\equiv 3\pmod 4$ for all $i\geq 1$.

**$a_i$ modulo 20.** For $i\geq 1$, $a_{i+1}\equiv 3^{a_i\bmod 4}\equiv 3^3=27\equiv 7\pmod{20}$. So $a_i\equiv 7\pmod{20}$ for all $i\geq 2$ (verified: $a_2=27\equiv 7$).

**$a_i$ modulo 100.** For $i\geq 2$, $a_{i+1}\equiv 3^{a_i\bmod 20}\equiv 3^7=2187\equiv 87\pmod{100}$. So $a_i\equiv 87\pmod{100}$ for all $i\geq 3$ (verified: $a_3=3^{27}\equiv 3^7\equiv 87\pmod{100}$, since $27\equiv 7\pmod{20}$).

**Conclusion.** The last-two-digit values are:
$$a_1\equiv 03,\quad a_2\equiv 27,\quad a_i\equiv 87\;\text{for all }i\geq 3.$$

The value $87$ occurs for all $i\geq 3$, hence infinitely often. Every other value in $\{00,\dots,99\}$ occurs at most finitely many times (at most once or twice). Therefore the unique integer between $00$ and $99$ inclusive that occurs as the last two digits of infinitely many $a_i$ is $\boxed{87}$.

